<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Minimal hardenedRuntime entitlements for WorkMax Desktop.

  Each entitlement is here for a specific reason. Notarization inspects this
  plist and rejects entitlements the binary's behavior does not justify, so
  "might need it later" is not a reason — it is a rejection.

  - com.apple.security.network.client:
      The sidecar makes outbound HTTPS calls to workmax.app (OAuth + Cloud
      Proxy). Note this covers outbound connections only.

  NOT included, and why each absence is deliberate:

    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory:
        These existed for Electron's V8, which emitted and executed JIT code.
        The Wails shell uses the operating system's WebView, whose JavaScript
        engine runs in a system process that carries its own entitlements. Our
        binary neither emits nor executes generated code, so both were removed
        when the Electron shell was retired (2026-08-08).

    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables:
        Electron's build-from-source workflows occasionally needed DYLD_*
        overrides. Nothing in the Wails build does: the shell locates its
        renderer and native assets through explicit paths derived from the
        executable location, not through the dynamic loader's environment.

    - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation:
        NOT NEEDED YET, and deliberately not added in advance. The local
        knowledge index dlopens an ONNX Runtime library that is downloaded to
        the user's data directory rather than shipped in the bundle, and a
        library signed by another team cannot be loaded under library
        validation. That milestone is where this entitlement gets justified —
        adding it now would weaken every build in the meantime for a feature
        that is not enabled. See
        ProjectDocs/wails-migration-evaluation-2026-08.md §0.5.6.

    - com.apple.security.network.server:
        We do accept connections, but only on the loopback interface, which
        macOS does not gate behind this entitlement. Adding it would claim a
        capability we do not want and cannot justify.

    - com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write:
        Chat attachments are read through the webview's own file input and
        handed to the sidecar in-process; the app never opens a path the user
        picked through a system panel.

    - com.apple.security.device.camera / microphone:
        No media capture. The webview is also served a Permissions-Policy that
        denies both, so this is refused in two places.

  Public distribution: paired with the codesign runtime option and an Apple
  Developer ID Application certificate, this plist satisfies notarytool.
  desktop/scripts/notarize-mac.sh runs the actual submission, and
  desktop/scripts/inspect-mac-package.sh pins this exact entitlement set so a
  speculative addition fails the build rather than surprising Apple.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
